      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Edit: solution equivalent to jfrank319\n\nWe see that the bottom left circle is tangent to the sides of the square, so the bottom left radius is equal to half the length from the corner to the side of the square (half the side). Similarly, we can make some basic circles that take into consideration the placement of the square and where the circles are tangent to the square and put the circles tangent to one another.\n\nThe radius from the center of the square to the bottom left corner will be the length of the square (.5 times side length). The distance from the center of the square to the center of the top left circle is (square side - circle radius on top left). These two lines form a triangle. By applying the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n[asy]\nsize(100);\nimport graph3;\n\nreal x,y,z;\npath p3(real x)\n{\ndraw((x+1,0)--(x-1,0));\ndraw((x,0)--(x,1));\ndraw((x,1)--(x+1,1));\nfill((x,1)--(x+1,1)--(x+1,0)--cycle);\nreturn (x,1);\n}\n\nfor (y = .5; y <= .89999999999998; y = y + .001) {\nx = -1/3 cos(y*6.28);\nz = root(2, 1/l*sqrt(3));\ndraw(\" \", p3(x), xscale=z);\ndraw(\" \", p3(x), legend=None);\n}\ndraw(\" \", p3(.001));\ndraw(\" \", p3(.89999999999998));\nfilldraw(circle((0,.5), .5), black);\nfilldraw(circle((-1/3,.8660254037844386), .5), black);\nlabel(\" \", (0,.5), ridgeline(180));\nlabel(\" \", (-1/3,.8660254037844386),ridgeline(180));\n[/asy]\n\n\t\t(5/3)*(.5)*1\nWhen combined with 1/2 from the above, we have\n\n\t\t(25/12)*(.5)*1\n\t\t(25/24)\n\nFinal answer: (25/24)",
